\title{Quickstart}
\meta{toc}{false}

\p{Define a function, give baux its parameter bounds, and alternate between proposing points and returning their values.}

\subtree{
  \title{Start with a Function}

  \p{
    This example searches six bounded parameters. \code{ask} proposes points; your function evaluates them; \code{tell} records the results. baux maximizes values, so return the negative of a loss you want to minimize.
  }

  \<div>[id]{quickstart}{
  \pre{
import numpy as np
import baux


def objective(x):
    # Coupled 6D bimodal Gaussian-ridge landscape (peak near x = 0.65)
    r1 = np.exp(-0.4 * np.sum((x - 0.65) ** 2, axis=1))
    r2 = 0.65 * np.exp(-0.5 * np.sum((x + 0.55) ** 2, axis=1))
    couple = -0.15 * np.sum((x[:, 1:] - 0.5 * x[:, :-1]) ** 2, axis=1)
    return r1 + r2 + couple


bounds = np.tile([-1.5, 1.5], (6, 1))
pipe = baux.enn(k=8) | baux.ucb(beta=1.5)
state = baux.init(bounds, pipe, key=baux.key(0))
zero_state = baux.init(bounds, baux.turbo_zero(num_init=12), key=baux.key(0))

for _ in range(18):
    state, batch = baux.ask(state, num_arms=4)
    state, _ = baux.tell(state, batch, objective(batch.points))
    zero_state, zb = baux.ask(zero_state, num_arms=4)
    zero_state, _ = baux.tell(zero_state, zb, objective(zb.points))

print("6D Bimodal Ridge @ 72 evals:")
print("  TuRBO-ENN :", round(float(state.best_y), 4), np.round(state.best_x, 3))
print("  TuRBO-Zero:", round(float(zero_state.best_y), 4))
  }
  }

  \p{
    \code{batch.points} has one row per proposed point. Return one value per row, or several columns for a vector objective. The pipeline above compares an ENN surrogate + UCB acquisition against model-free \code{turbo_zero} across the same evaluation budget; [the configuration reference](api-pipelines.html#baux.enn) describes their options.
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{Use It for Your Problem}

  \<div>[id]{guides}{
  \ul{
    \li{[Structured parameters](baux-0008.html) — Search dictionaries and dataclasses containing log scales, angles, mixtures and discrete choices. Read the proposed values from \code{batch.params}.}
    \li{[Multiple regions and workers](baux-0009.html) — Explore several centers, return evaluations as workers finish, and save or resume a search.}
    \li{[Surrogate predictions](baux-0003.html) — Fit ENN to existing observations, query predictions and draw samples.}
    \li{[Interactive examples](simulation.html) — Explore synthetic objectives and the tokamak control example.}
  }
  }

  \p{
    See the [API reference](docs.html) for signatures, array shapes and configuration options. The [source repository](https://github.com/Kvutza/baux) contains the Rust implementation and Python frontend.
  }
